    <string-array name="weather_uv_advice">
        <item>No relevant UV radiation, no sun protection needed.</item>
        <item>Low UV exposure. No protection needed for most people.</item>
        <item>Moderate UV exposure. Use sun protection during longer stays outdoors.</item>
        <item>High UV exposure. Sun protection, hat and sunglasses recommended, avoid midday sun.</item>
        <item>Very high UV exposure. Prefer shade, do not stay in the sun for long between 11 and 16.</item>
        <item>Extreme UV exposure. Keep time outdoors as short as possible, full protection needed.</item>
    </string-array>
    <string-array name="weather_moon_astronomy">
        <item>The moon is between Earth and Sun, its lit side faces away from Earth. It is not visible.</item>
        <item>A thin crescent appears in the west shortly after sunset and sets soon after.</item>
        <item>First quarter: the right half is lit. Moonrise around noon, moonset around midnight.</item>
        <item>More than half is lit. The moon rises in the afternoon and stays visible into the night.</item>
        <item>Earth is between Sun and Moon. The whole visible side is lit. Moonrise at sunset, moonset at sunrise.</item>
        <item>The moon is waning and rises later each night. It is still in the sky in the morning.</item>
        <item>Last quarter: the left half is lit. Moonrise around midnight, moonset around noon.</item>
        <item>A thin crescent is visible in the east shortly before sunrise. A new cycle begins soon.</item>
    </string-array>
